 Constructive Subscriber Feedback & Review
As an active subscriber using pyvisualiser for desktop widgets, here is my honest architectural and aesthetic review of what works brilliantly and where the API can be evolved even further:

🟢 What is Exceptionally Good
Shader Injection & Background System (BackgroundStyle(shader=...)): Being able to swap out complex raymarching shaders like liquidorb, warping, or starvis as a background layer with a single line of Python is incredible. It instantly gives desktop widgets a professional VJ-software tier polish.

ColFramer / RowFramer Compositing: Building multi-panel layouts feels natural and declarative. The percentage-based relative scaling means windows resize or adapt to different display aspect ratios without breaking offsets.

Additive Bloom & Layer Separation (Phase 2 Success): Because UI text and vectors now render after the bloom composite pass, glowing elements (like the neon spectrum tips and needle highlights) bloom gorgeously without polluting or blurring the typography.

🟡 What Could Be Even Better (Future Enhancements)
Dynamic Layout Margin / Gap Support in Framers:

Current: ColFramer and RowFramer divide space evenly or via ratios with a percentage padding (padpc).

Improvement: Adding explicit pixel-gap support (e.g., gap=10 in pixels) alongside percentage ratios would make fixed-size desktop widgets much easier to align against fixed-width UI controls (like a 64px volume knob).

Shader Uniform Auto-Binding:

Current: Shaders require manual uniform checks (if 'u_vu' in self.prog: ...) in ShaderFrame or BackgroundRenderPass.

Improvement: A standardized uniform injection dictionary where audio state variables (vu, bass, treble, bpm, centroid, flux) are bulk-injected automatically into any active GLSL program would drastically clean up custom shader writing.

Window Opacity & Click-Through Flags:

Target: Desktop widgets.

Improvement: Exposing window hints in GraphicsDriverGL (such as setting an SDL window hint for window transparency/alpha blending or click-through window flags) would allow pyvisualiser to run as a true borderless, transparent floating desktop widget over wallpaper background apps.